if SYS_BOARD = "rpi"

config RPI_EFI_NR_SPIN_PAGES
	int "Spin table page count"
	default 1
	help
	  Number of pages to reserve starting at page 0 for spin tables in the EFI
	  memory map

config CMD_BOOTCIRCLE
	bool "bootcircle - start a Circle bare metal application"
	depends on ARM64
	help
	  Circle (https://github.com/rsta2/circle) is a C++ bare metal
	  environment for the Raspberry Pi.  Its kernel image is a raw binary
	  that has to be entered at 0x80000 with the MMU and caches off, so
	  neither booti nor go can start it.

	  Say Y here to get a bootcircle command that reproduces the hand-off
	  the Raspberry Pi armstub performs, including the device tree pointer
	  Circle expects at address 0xF8 and, on BCM2712, the repair of the
	  resident EL3 firmware that Circle's multi-core support needs.

config RPI_UART0_CONSOLE
	bool "Console on UART0 (GPIO 14/15) instead of UART10 on BCM2712"
	depends on ARM64 && PL01X_SERIAL
	help
	  The firmware device tree puts the Raspberry Pi 5 console on uart10,
	  the PL011 in the BCM2712, which comes out of the 3-pin debug UART
	  header next to the HDMI ports.  A Circle application as shipped uses
	  UART0 of the RP1 southbridge instead, on GPIO 14/15 - pins 8 and 10
	  of the 40-pin header - so U-Boot and the payload need two different
	  cables.

	  Say Y here to move the U-Boot console to UART0 as well, so that one
	  cable sees the whole boot.  U-Boot muxes GPIO 14/15, binds a PL011
	  at RP1 UART0 (0x1f00030000, 50 MHz) and drops the stdout-path the
	  firmware provides.  Other Raspberry Pi models are unaffected: the
	  console only moves when U-Boot is running on a BCM2712.

	  This relies on the firmware having mapped RP1 at 0x1f00000000, the
	  address it uses for USB and network boot and the one Circle and
	  Linux expect.  If a firmware ever leaves RP1 unmapped there is no
	  console at all, so say N to go back to the debug UART header.

endif
